  "articleSummary": "The article argues that Google's self-driving cars are involved in more accidents than human drivers, but virtually all are caused by human drivers unfamiliar with robotic behavior. The core thesis is that programming cars to obey traffic laws absolutely creates safety and ethical dilemmas (the trolley problem), such as whether robots should break rules to avoid collisions. It proposes a three-tier perception system that adds flexible adjustments in traffic and extreme collision avoidance, balancing rule-following with practical safety. The tone is analytical and forward-looking, targeting tech enthusiasts and policy makers, with recurring metaphors of legal liability and the \"octogenarian\" driver framing.",
